  "article_opening": "How Nvidia Became The Black Sheep Of The Chip Stock Rally\nNVIDIA’s fundamentals have never looked stronger, yet its stock is limping through 2026. NVIDIA (NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction) is up just 3.2% year to date, a striking laggard in a sector where money is flooding into almost everything else.…\nBy Danielle Liverance\nNVDA is up just 7% year to date despite 85% revenue growth to $82B, punished for being priced as the consensus AI trade.\nAMD surged 171% and Micron 305% year to date, rewa",
  "passage": "…DIA shares are under pressure on reports of delays across their upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems.\nThe earnings do not explain the tape. Revenue rose 85% year over year to $81.6 billion, with data center up 92% to $75.2 billion. CEO Jensen Huang framed it plainly: “Agentic AI has arrived, doing productive work, generating real value and scaling rapidly across companies and industries.” But NVDA trades at a forward P/E of less than 20 now, and when a name becomes the consensus AI trade, even a blowout can underwhelm a market that had already priced perfection. Polymarket sentiment tracks this: end-of-July close-above-$200 probability sits at just 43%.\nThe narrative is fragmenting. NVIDIA still owns roughly 81% of the AI chip market , but Broadcom’s custom ASICs for Alphabet and Meta are eating the story, with Bloomberg Intelligence forecasting a 27% CAGR for custom ASICs through 2033 versus 16% for AI accelerators. Broadcom guided Q3 AI semiconductor revenue to $16.0 billion, more than 200% year over year. AMD’s Meta Instinct GPU deal anchors a multi-year hyperscaler thesis , and Lisa Su noted “leading customer forecasts exceeding our initial expectations” on MI450.\nOne important number to watch is the relative size of AMD compared to NVIDIA. Right now, AMD is worth about 18% of NVIDIA’s value. At the beginning of the year, AMD was worth less than 10% NVIDIA. Investors are still piling into companies benefiting from t…",
  "statement": "NVIDIA still owns roughly 81% of the AI chip market"
 },
 {
  "id": "G190",
  "article_id": "nvblog:nations-deploy-ai-strategic-priorities",
  "title": "How Nations Are Deploying AI for Strategic Priorities | NVIDIA Blog",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Countries are building and running artificial intelligence using national infrastructure, data, talent and business networks, in ways that fit their language, culture and regulations.\nNations have long invested in domestic infrastructure to advance their economies, protect and use their data, and take advantage of technology opportunities in areas such as transportation, communications, commerce, entertainment and healthcare.\nAI, the most important technology of our time, is turbocharging innova",
  "passage": "…zens.\nSince 2019, NVIDIA’s AI Nations initiative has helped countries in every region build out their AI ecosystems and workforce, creating the conditions for engineers, developers, scientists, entrepreneurs, creators and public sector officials to pursue their AI ambitions at home.\nIn Europe, AI agents from ThinkDeep , built on the NVIDIA AI platform, are helping France’s Ministry of Economy and Finance automate complex public‑service workflows by processing millions of documents and data sources, cutting document search times from two days to two minutes, saving 2 million euros for 10,000 employees and reducing energy use through more efficient, in‑country infrastructure control.\nIn Asia, India’s Sarvam platform , powered by NVIDIA GPUs and built entirely on domestic infrastructure, is delivering multilingual AI models and voice agents optimized for the country’s 22 official languages, enabling government and enterprise services to reach hundreds of millions of people in their own languages while keeping data, compute and governance under national control.\nIn Latin America, AI solutions from Widelabs , running on NVIDIA‑accelerated infrastructure, are helping modernize and expand access to legal services for the Public Ministry of Rio Grande do Sul in Brazil, streamlining internal investigations and making justice records easier to find and use for more than 8 million citizens across nearly 500 municipalities — aligning advanced computing with more efficient, transparent and inclusive public administration.\nThese efforts …",
  "statement": "India’s Sarvam platform , powered by NVIDIA GPUs and built entirely on domestic infrastructure, is delivering multilingual AI models and voice agents"
 },
 {
  "id": "G522",
  "article_id": "gnews:b3dd2a0f914f65ea3ebf2914",
  "title": "NVIDIA Run:ai Is Now Open Source as Part of NVIDIA DSX AI Factory Platform | NVIDIA Technical Blog",
  "publication_day": "2026-10-13",
  "article_opening": "Accelerate innovation across the DSX ecosystem and bring AI factory solutions to market faster with the open foundation for AI workload and GPU orchestration.\nBy Ekin Karabulut and Sam Heywood\nNVIDIA is releasing the full NVIDIA Run:ai platform as open source software, expanding the NVIDIA DSX AI Factory Platform with a foundation for AI workload and GPU orchestration.\nThe release includes four modular components that can be adopted independently: KAI Scheduler for GPU-aware Kubernetes schedulin",
  "passage": "… Streamer has been adopted across the inference stack, including by inference engines, cloud providers and storage platform vendors.\nModel Streamer is integrated upstream in vLLM and SGLang , where it can be enabled with the --load-format runai_streamer flag, as well as in ModelExpress . Support covers local filesystems, S3-compatible stores, and network file shares alongside several cloud backends detailed below.\nMajor cloud providers now document Model Streamer in their own guidance.\nAWS : Model Streamer ships in the vLLM Deep Learning Container and is the recommended fast path in the official EKS documentation for running AI/ML inference , with tuned concurrency and chunk sizing for S3 .\nGoogle Cloud : Native Cloud Storage support built in collaboration with NVIDIA , including GKE documentation for streaming straight from a bucket into accelerator memory.\nMicrosoft Azure : Azure Blob Storage integration, jointly developed by Microsoft and NVIDIA Run:ai to provide first-class model streaming for Azure customers. Published benchmarks on eight NVIDIA H100 VMs shows 3x-6x faster loading than the default loader and a steady 80 Gbps, compared with approximately 40 Gbps for the default path.\nSeveral AI data infrastructure partners have independently benchmarked Model Streamer and published their results:\nWEKA loaded into GPU memory in just 3.5 seconds, roughly 40% faster than loading from local NVMe—enabling organizations to build petabyte-scale shared model…",
  "statement": "Google Cloud : Native Cloud Storage support built in collaboration with NVIDIA"
 },
 {
  "id": "G267",
  "article_id": "gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57",
  "title": "NVIDIA DSX Promises More Revenue per Gigawatt. Who Actually Captures It?",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "NVIDIA’s DSX reference design and operating system are engineered to raise the revenue per gigawatt an AI factory can produce by lifting tokens per megawatt. Paired with NVIDIA’s July 1 revenue-sharing model, DSX forms half of a two-part business model: grow the revenue a gigawatt generates, then collect a recurring cut of it. Sharon AI and Firmus are building the first DSX-aligned factories under the arrangement.\nHow NVIDIA’s DSX reference design and operating system raise revenue per gigawatt ",
  "passage": "… of it, and it is consistent with the message Kress has carried on recent earnings calls.\nDSX Reframes the Gigawatt From a Power Budget to a Revenue Function\nDSX is not a single product but a stack: reference designs for physical blueprint, DSX Sim as an Omniverse digital twin that validates a factory before a rack lands, DSX OS for the open source operations software that runs it, and the MaxLPS efficiency layer that tunes power at the rack. The layer that matters most for revenue per gigawatt is MaxLPS, which combines liquid cooling with software-driven in-rack power tuning to run up to 40% more GPUs at their most efficient operating point inside a fixed power budget, on NVIDIA’s figures. DSX Flex extends the same control to the utility grid, with a multi-megawatt pilot alongside Emerald AI and Silicon Valley Power that adjusts factory consumption programmatically in response to grid signals. DSX reframes AI factory competition from cores per dollar to tokens per megawatt. That reframing is the entire revenue-per-gigawatt case: if power is the binding constraint, the operator that extracts more tokens per watt earns more revenue, and DSX is the tool NVIDIA is selling to do it.\nThe Business Model Has Two Halves: Grow Revenue per Gigawatt, Then Share It\nThe connection to the financing model is where the strategy becomes coherent. Under the July 1 revenue-sharing and credit-support model, NVIDIA earns its standard hardware margin plus a recurring, usage-linked share of the cloud income the supported capa…",
  "statement": "DSX Flex extends the same control to the utility grid, with a multi-megawatt pilot alongside Emerald AI and Silicon Valley Power"
 },
 {
  "id": "G084",
  "article_id": "gnews:f8d63eab0337895e8e95cf02",
  "title": "Nvidia Uses The Specter of Huawei to Make Its Chip Exports Case",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-02",
  "article_opening": "Before Nvidia won approval to sell more chips to China, it told the U.S. government that Huawei could have enough chips to satisfy global demand.\nBy Noah Berman\nWhen Nvidia pitched the Trump administration last year to let it sell advanced AI chips to China, the world’s biggest chipmaker issued a clear warning: Huawei, its most formidable Chinese rival, could soon dominate the global industry.\nIn a report that Nvidia presented to the U.S. government — which The Wire has reviewed — the company wa",
  "passage": "…i, its most formidable Chinese rival, could soon dominate the global industry.\nIn a report that Nvidia presented to the U.S. government — which The Wire has reviewed — the company warned that Huawei’s stockpile of chips, along with growing capacity at its manufacturing partner Semiconductor Manufacturing International Corp. (SMIC), give it “the flexibility to satisfy global AI chip demand.”\n“If Nvidia is kept out of the China market, Huawei production and sales will soar,” the report said, although it did not specify a timeframe or refer to the differences in performance between Nvidia and Huawei chips.\nThe pitch — alongside Nvidia Chief Executive Jensen Huang’s personal diplomacy — worked. President Trump announced that his administration would allow Nvidia to sell its H200 chips to China last December. The government had previously restricted Nvidia from selling China the chip, which it launched in 2023, due to export controls the Biden administration put in place.\nNvidia’s analysis sheds light on how the world’s most valuable company has presented Huawei’s growing capabilities as justification for it to be able to sell more to China, the world’s second-largest market for chips after the U.S.\nNvidia is attempting to “scare U.S. policymakers that Huawei is this great big threat,” says Gregory Allen , an expert on semiconductor export controls. “They are, but not in the way Nvidia is claiming.”\n“[But] there is no apples-to-apples comparison between Nvidia chips and Huawei chips,” says Alle…",
  "statement": "President Trump announced that his administration would allow Nvidia to sell its H200 chips to China last December"
 },
 {
  "id": "G399",
  "article_id": "gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c",
  "title": "China to let AI firms buy Nvidia H200s, says report",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-08",
  "article_opening": "China to let AI firms buy Nvidia H200s, says report\nNvidia’s H200 hails from the company’s Hopper line of processors used for training and running AI models.\nChina will allow top AI companies like Alibaba and ByteDance to buy limited amounts of Nvidia's H200 AI chips, easing previous restrictions.\nChinese firms must request approval specifying the number of chips and reasons for purchase, with a possible total below 200,000.\nThe move responds to high AI demand but aims to protect China's chip in",
  "passage": "…Beijing has also cited fears that allowing American-made chips into the local market could pose a cybersecurity risk.\nBut soaring demand for AI processors helped spur the Chinese authorities to allow some H200 purchases, according to the Information, citing two unidentified people familiar with the matter.\nLabs in China have been struggling, like their US counterparts, to find enough computing power to meet their growing needs to train and run new models.\nNvidia’s H200 hails from the company’s Hopper line of processors used for training and running AI models. It was the most powerful AI chip on the market until the introduction of the company’s next-generation Blackwell models in late 2024.\nNvidia is now working to release an even more powerful line-up called Rubin in the second half of 2026 . Washington has continued to restrict sales of more capable AI chips to China on national security grounds.\nThe Chinese government, meanwhile, is still figuring out how many H200 chips to allow the companies to buy, but the total may end up being fewer than 200,000, the Information reported. When it comes to AI infrastructure, that is not a large number. A single data centre can use more than 400,000 Blackwell processors.\nIn May, Nvidia chief financial officer Colette Kress said the H200 chips had yet to generate any revenue for the company in China.\n“We are uncertain whether any imports will be allowed into the country,” she said.",
  "statement": "Nvidia is now working to release an even more powerful line-up called Rubin in the second half of 2026 ."
 },
 {
  "id": "G056",
  "article_id": "gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816",
  "title": "Nvidia offers to take a cut of AI cloud revenue on top of hardware sales in new optional financing vehicle — trades tokens for revenue cut",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-02",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia offers to take a cut of AI cloud revenue on top of hardware sales in new optional financing vehicle — trades tokens for revenue cut\nBy Luke James\nSharon AI and Firmus are the first partners, with a combined total of up to 210,000 GPUs.\nNvidia has announced a new business model under which it’ll be able to double-dip for revenue on the same silicon: once when its partner AI clouds use its hardware, and again as an ongoing percentage of the revenue that hardware generates. In a blog post co",
  "passage": "…ntage of the revenue that hardware generates. In a blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress, the company pitched the “revenue-sharing and credit-support model” as a way to open compute access to startups that can’t finance it themselves. In practice, cash-poor AI companies trade a slice of whatever they eventually earn for tokens today, while a supplier already running roughly 75% gross margins reaches into its customers’ income statements for a second helping of cash. Australia's Sharon AI and Singapore-based Firmus Technologies are the first named partners.\nUnder the structure, participating AI clouds procure Nvidia infrastructure and sell Nvidia-powered cloud services to end customers. Nvidia collects its usual product revenue on the hardware plus a percentage of the cloud income earned on that capacity, which the blog post describes as a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream. Per Bloomberg , developers receive token credits in exchange for a slice of their future sales, but neither Nvidia nor its partners has disclosed the revenue-split percentages.\nIt’s no secret that the credit-support side of this model will help to address a financing gap that Nvidia itself has identified. Even signed, long-term customer commitments have failed to convince lenders to fund large-scale deployments, leaving smaller clouds unable to borrow against the demand they had already generated.\nIn an 8-K filing dated June 12th, Sharon AI disclosed that the agreement runs for six years and covers 72 MW of new Aus…",
  "statement": "Nvidia collects its usual product revenue on the hardware plus a percentage of the cloud income earned on that capacity"
 },
 {
  "id": "G261",
  "article_id": "gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32",
  "title": "How Nvidia Became The Black Sheep Of The Chip Stock Rally",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "How Nvidia Became The Black Sheep Of The Chip Stock Rally\nNVIDIA’s fundamentals have never looked stronger, yet its stock is limping through 2026. NVIDIA (NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction) is up just 3.2% year to date, a striking laggard in a sector where money is flooding into almost everything else.…\nBy Danielle Liverance\nNVDA is up just 7% year to date despite 85% revenue growth to $82B, punished for being priced as the consensus AI trade.\nAMD surged 171% and Micron 305% year to date, rewa",
  "passage": "…n perfection already baked in.\nNvidia's $119B in supply commitments hinge on AI delivering measurable economic value beyond productivity tools, which is a question the market appears to already be pricing in.\nNVIDIA’s fundamentals have never looked stronger, yet its stock is limping through 2026. NVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction ) is up just 3.2% year to date, a striking laggard in a sector where money is flooding into almost everything else. That is the paradox: the company at the center of the AI buildout has become the black sheep of the chip rally.\nAdvanced Micro Devices ( NASDAQ:AMD ) has ripped 171.25% year to date. Micron Technology ( NASDAQ:MU ) is up a stunning 304.62%. Intel ( NASDAQ:INTC ), still deep in a turnaround under Lip-Bu Tan, has surged 278.4% on DCAI momentum and NVIDIA’s own $5 billion equity investment. Broadcom ( NASDAQ:AVGO ), the custom-silicon story, is one example of another megacap stock that largely matches NVIDIA’s returns.\nBut the broader story is one of semiconductor stocks rallying. The VanEck Semiconductor ETF (Nasdaq: SMH) is up 59% year-to-date. The divergence looks set to continue today. The VanEck is up 3% premarket while NVIDIA shares are up just .3%, as of 8:40 a.m. ET. NVIDIA shares are under pressure on reports of delays across their upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems.\nThe earnings do not explain the tape. Revenue rose 85% year over year to $81.6 billion, with data center up 92% to $75.2 billion. CEO Jensen Huang framed it plainly: “Agentic AI has arrived, doi…",
  "statement": "Intel ( NASDAQ:INTC ), still deep in a turnaround under Lip-Bu Tan, has surged 278.4% on DCAI momentum and NVIDIA’s own $5 billion equity investment."
 },
 {
  "id": "G340",
  "article_id": "nvblog:nvidia-vera-max-single-threaded-cpu-at-scale",
  "title": "AI Innovators Adopt NVIDIA Vera — Why Max Single-Threaded CPU at Scale Matters | NVIDIA Blog",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-07",
  "article_opening": "AI Innovators Adopt NVIDIA Vera — Why Max Single-Threaded CPU at Scale Matters | NVIDIA Blog\nAI Innovators Adopt NVIDIA Vera — Why Max Single-Threaded CPU at Scale Matters\nNVIDIA Vera exemplifies a new class of CPU, architected for the era of agents and being adopted by AI innovators including Perplexity; NVIDIA CPU roadmap continues with the NVIDIA Rosa CPU and its Rigel core.\nMax single-threaded CPUs at scale are a new category of CPUs built for the agentic AI era.\nA cross the creation and dep",
  "passage": "…th the full memory performance of the CPU without creating bottlenecks that slows down every core.\nThe result is faster agent loops. In loaded CPU workloads that represent agentic execution, Vera delivers 1.8x the sustained per-core performance of x86.\nThose gains compound across tool calls, code executions, data-processing steps and verification passes, helping AI factories complete more agent work with the GPUs they already operate.\nPerplexity tested Vera on the agentic work it runs every day. Running a real coding workflow — cloning a repository and running its test suite in sandboxes — Vera completed the job about 1.5x faster than x86, and started concurrent sandboxes up to 1.9x faster. Perplexity is now looking to deploy Vera in its upcoming production system.\nAgents also depend on data. They query, retrieve, filter and move information constantly, and Vera runs those CPU-side data workloads faster. Partners have measured 3x faster large-scale SQL analytics with Starburst and up to 6x lower latency on real-time streaming with Redpanda, both against leading x86 server CPUs.\nAgent work isn’t one workload. An agent runs tools and sandboxes, processes data, serves requests and trains the next model with reinforcement learning — and all of it leans on the same strengths.\nOne Vera handles the whole range, rather than requiring a different CPU for each kind of work. And because Vera is the same CPU that hosts the GPUs in NVIDIA Vera Rubin and powers th…",
  "statement": "Perplexity is now looking to deploy Vera in its upcoming production system"
 },
 {
  "id": "G504",
  "article_id": "gnews:488fd6e4ba9177df77a2fed2",
  "title": "SpaceX Wants to Borrow $40 Billion to Buy Nvidia Chips",
  "publication_day": "2026-10-07",
  "article_opening": "SpaceX is borrowing $40 billion for NVIDIA chips while posting a $541 million quarterly loss on capex more than double its revenue.\nApollo Global Management is expected to lead the deal, as Morgan Stanley warns AI infrastructure will need $1.5 trillion in outside financing by 2028.\nLockup expirations will release up to 1.3 billion SpaceX shares after Q3 earnings, pressuring stock as insiders sell right after a post-IPO rally.\nA recent report citing people familiar with the matter said SpaceX ( N",
  "passage": "SpaceX is borrowing $40 billion for NVIDIA chips while posting a $541 million quarterly loss on capex more than double its revenue.\nApollo Global Management is expected to lead the deal, as Morgan Stanley warns AI infrastructure will need $1.5 trillion in outside financing by 2028.\nLockup expirations will release up to 1.3 billion SpaceX shares after Q3 earnings, pressuring stock as insiders sell right after a post-IPO rally.\nA recent report citing people familiar with the matter said SpaceX ( NASDAQ:SPCX ) is seeking about $40 billion of financing to pay for an order of AI chips from NVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA ). In after-hours trading, SpaceX fell about 1%, and NVIDIA rose about 0.5%.\nUnder the reported plan, SpaceX would take on the debt to fund the purchase, while NVIDIA would record the chip order as revenue when it ships.\nSpaceX, Apollo Global Management ( NYSE:APO ) and NVIDIA did not immediately respond to requests for comment. The financing remains at the discussion stage.\nWhat SpaceX Has Committed Versus What It Only Seeks\nSpaceX is reportedly seeking about $10 billion in bank loans and about $30 billion in investment-grade debt. Investment-grade means bonds rated as having low default risk, which pension funds and insurance companies can hold.\nThe bank loans are the easier part, because a…",
  "statement": "SpaceX ( NASDAQ:SPCX ) is seeking about $40 billion of financing to pay for an order of AI chips from NVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA )"
 },
 {
  "id": "G045",
  "article_id": "gnews:917648d49b17070ea08fcdc1",
  "title": "Nvidia recruits longtime Microsoft sales leader Nick Parker with $40M+ pay package",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-02",
  "article_opening": "Nick Parker, a 26-year Microsoft veteran who led the company’s worldwide commercial sales business, is leaving to become Nvidia’s new sales chief — a high-profile talent shift between two of the biggest players in the AI boom.\nParker will join Nvidia as executive vice president of worldwide field operations, effective Aug. 24, according to a regulatory filing . He succeeds Jay Puri, who is retiring after 21 years running Nvidia’s global sales operation and will stay on as a senior adviser.\n“Micr",
  "passage": "Nick Parker, a 26-year Microsoft veteran who led the company’s worldwide commercial sales business, is leaving to become Nvidia’s new sales chief — a high-profile talent shift between two of the biggest players in the AI boom.\nParker will join Nvidia as executive vice president of worldwide field operations, effective Aug. 24, according to a regulatory filing . He succeeds Jay Puri, who is retiring after 21 years running Nvidia’s global sales operation and will stay on as a senior adviser.\n“Microsoft and NVIDIA are great partners and I look forward to continuing to nurture that fantastic relationship,” Parker wrote in a LinkedIn post announcing the move.\nThe regulatory filing by Nvidia sets Parker’s base salary in the new role at $1 million, with a $5 million signing bonus and equity grants targeted at $40 million. The bulk of that, $35 million in restricted stock units, vests over roughly four years, while the additional $5 million in shares is tied to Nvidia outperforming the S&P 500 over three years.\nThe new role puts him in charge of global sales and customer relationships at the center of the AI boom, reporting directly to Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang — one of the most consequential commercial roles in the industry, overseeing the operation that sells Nvidia’s chips to the world’s largest companies.\nParker, 55, rose through OEM, device and partner sales roles at Microsoft before being named president of industry and partner sales in 2022. After a promotion this year, he served most recently as executi…",
  "statement": "The regulatory filing by Nvidia sets Parker’s base salary in the new role at $1 million, with a $5 million signing bonus and equity grants targeted at $40 million."
 },
 {
  "id": "G192",
  "article_id": "gnews:98a4e8ff0c0260d8dc9e531d",
  "title": "Nvidia pushes Kyber release to 2028 following manufacturing concerns – report",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "By Charlotte Trueman\nNvidia has delayed production of its Kyber rack-scale architecture by more than 12 months, pushing its release date to 2028, SemiAnalysis has reported.\nThe Kyber server cabinets, which have been designed to house 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs connected via the company’s NVLink 7 Switch, had originally been slated for release in 2027.\nKyber allows GPUs to be mounted in compute trays that sit vertically rather than horizontally, a design that Nvidia has described as being “like books o",
  "passage": "By Charlotte Trueman\nNvidia has delayed production of its Kyber rack-scale architecture by more than 12 months, pushing its release date to 2028, SemiAnalysis has reported.\nThe Kyber server cabinets, which have been designed to house 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs connected via the company’s NVLink 7 Switch, had originally been slated for release in 2027.\nKyber allows GPUs to be mounted in compute trays that sit vertically rather than horizontally, a design that Nvidia has described as being “like books on a shelf,” and which reportedly boosts density and reduces latency.\nHowever, the research firm claimed that issues in manufacturing the printed circuit board (PCB) midplane that connects the modules together have caused the setback.\n“Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,” Sem…",
  "statement": "Nvidia has delayed production of its Kyber rack-scale architecture by more than 12 months, pushing its release date to 2028, SemiAnalysis has reported."
 },
 {
  "id": "G378",
  "article_id": "gnews:295fceb25273abae9bb6c995",
  "title": "A Circuit Board Problem Just Delayed Nvidia's Next AI System to 2028 And Chip Stocks Are Already Feeling the Fallout",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-07",
  "article_opening": "A Circuit Board Problem Just Delayed Nvidia's Next AI System to 2028 And Chip Stocks Are Already Feeling the Fallout - 24/7 Wall St.\nA Circuit Board Problem Just Delayed Nvidia’s Next AI System to 2028 And Chip Stocks Are Already Feeling the Fallout\nA printed circuit board just pushed NVIDIA’s most powerful AI system into 2028 — at least according to one research firm. Semiconductor research firm SemiAnalysis reported on July 5–6, 2026 that NVIDIA’s Kyber NVL144 rack-scale AI system has been del",
  "passage": "…ayers with signal integrity, thermal tolerance, and yield at volume is a hard engineering problem, and SemiAnalysis frames it as the gating item for NVIDIA’s most ambitious system.\nThe delay raises a broader question: whether NVIDIA’s annual cadence, built to stay ahead of AMD and custom silicon, is hitting physical manufacturing limits. Data Center revenue reached $75.246 billion (+92% YoY) in Q1 FY27, and CEO Jensen Huang described “the largest infrastructure expansion in human history”. The bottleneck is assembly.\nAMD ( NASDAQ:AMD ) has the clearest window. Shares are up 157.77% year to date, and Goldman Sachs raised its AMD price target to $640 from $450 on July 6. CEO Lisa Su has said “customer engagement around MI450 Series and Helios is strengthening, with leading customer forecasts exceeding our initial expectations.”\nBroadcom ( NASDAQ:AVGO ) benefits through custom ASICs. Q2 AI semiconductor revenue was $10.8 billion, +143% YoY, with Q3 guided to $16.0 billion. Bloomberg Intelligence forecasts 27% CAGR for the custom ASIC market through 2033.\nGoogle ( NASDAQ:GOOGL ) is both a Rubin customer and a TPU competitor. Google Cloud revenue rose 63% with backlog nearly doubling to over $460 billion, and 2026 capex is guided up tp $190 billion. A Kyber slip strengthens the in-house silicon case.\nNVIDIA has not confirmed the delay. The affected product is a 2028 item; Blackwell and current Rubin systems remain in short supply. NVIDIA still commands approximately 81% of the AI chip market , and GuruFocus analysi…",
  "statement": "customer engagement around MI450 Series and Helios is strengthening, with leading customer forecasts exceeding our initial expectations"
 },
 {
  "id": "G346",
  "article_id": "nvblog:hugging-face-lerobot-models-frameworks-open-robotics",
  "title": "NVIDIA and Hugging Face Bring New Models and Frameworks to LeRobot for the Open Robotics Community | NVIDIA Blog",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-07",
  "article_opening": "New LeRobot integrations give developers open access to NVIDIA Isaac GR00T 1.7, Isaac Teleop, datasets and robotics workflows, with NVIDIA Cosmos 3 integration planned to bring frontier world models to open robotics development.\nOpen source AI has shown how quickly developers can innovate when models, data and tools are shared. Robotics has the same opportunity, but advancements in physical AI development can still be gated by costly and fragmented resources, from large datasets and robot founda",
  "passage": "…t open source physical AI dataset , downloaded more than 15 million times, which includes more than 350,000 real and simulated trajectories and 57 million grasps to help developers kickstart their robotics workflows.\nNVIDIA Isaac Sim – and Isaac Lab -based simulation frameworks to help developers set up environments, generate robot data, test policies and validate behaviors before moving to physical robots.\nNVIDIA Isaac Lab-Arena in LeRobot Environment Hub to enable developers to quickly prototype complex simulation environments, register them in LeRobot EnvHub and seamlessly use them within the LeRobot ecosystem to train and evaluate generalist robot policies such as GR00T, Pi and SmolVLA.\nNVIDIA Jetson Thor integration with LeRobot’s Reachy 2 to support deployment of VLA models on open source humanoid robots.\nLearn more about how to use Isaac Teleop, Isaac GR00T 1.7 and Isaac Lab-Arena with LeRobot for end-to-end humanoid development and explore detailed LeRobot integration workflows .",
  "statement": "NVIDIA Jetson Thor integration with LeRobot’s Reachy 2 to support deployment of VLA models on open source humanoid robots."
 },
 {
