 {
  "id": "G281",
  "article_id": "gnews:54aa9ce326115cedf8857512",
  "title": "CAPITAL IDEAS: The lag 7 — Is Micron the new Nvidia?",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "My rule of thumb, which I borrowed from DataTrek, is that a double is a bubble. If a company’s stock price doubles within a year, there is a good chance the price has advanced much faster than the fundamentals.\nBig Tech leadership is shifting. The Magnificent 7 and Broadcom weakened in June, while the broader market held up better than expected and Micron surged.\nInvestors should respect both the opportunity and the risk. Micron’s growth, margins, and role in AI memory are impressive, but its ra",
  "passage": "… even fatter margins.\nThat is why investors are watching closely.\nThe broader capital-spending backdrop supports the rally. Goldman Sachs estimates annual AI infrastructure capital expenditures could reach $765 billion in 2026 and grow to $1.6 trillion by 2031, with cumulative spending of about $7.6 trillion from 2026 through 2031. That spending includes chips, data centers, power, cooling, networking, and the other plumbing required to run AI at scale. Memory is one of those essential pieces of plumbing.\nUnfortunately, the AI boom is not just pushing up prices for the winners. It could also raise costs for everyone else. A shortage of memory might affect the prices of consumer electronics.\nApple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs. When Micron’s profit margins grow, it means higher costs for others. If memory companies earn more, then device makers, server makers, cloud providers, and eventually consumers could end up paying more for electronics.\nSo, is Micron the new Nvidia?\nMemory is now a key asset in the AI economy. But when a stock doubles or more, the risk goes up, even if the business is strong. However, ignoring Micron just because it has risen sharply could be the same mistake many made with Nvidia. Position size and diversification are important.\nNvidia is still the main name in AI, at least until companies like OpenAI and Anthropic go public. Micron and other memory chip makers are becoming key players in t…",
  "statement": "Apple said it can no longer absorb the surge in memory and storage costs and announced price increases for some Macs and iPads, citing higher memory costs."
 },
 {
  "id": "G188",
  "article_id": "gnews:4a892d75e355bee00aa3c572",
  "title": "Nvidia vs. Palantir: Which Artificial Intelligence (AI) Stock Is the Better Buy Right Now?",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "The artificial intelligence (AI) investment opportunity has become more measured throughout 2026. After years of parabolic gains driven by explosive demand for generative models and the underlying infrastructure that supports them, both Nvidia ( NVDA +2.48% ) and Palantir Technologies ( PLTR +1.34% ) have been relatively subdued stocks so far this year.\nWith that said, I think the muted stock performance of Nvidia and Palantir reflects broader digestion of lofty expectations rather than a fundam",
  "passage": "…which of these two AI powerhouses offers the more compelling risk-reward profile on a dip. Let's dig in and find out.\nNvidia vs. Palantir: Which company is growing faster?\nA close look at the financial results between Nvidia and Palantir highlights meaningful differences in both scale and momentum.\nDuring the first quarter of calendar 2026, Nvidia generated record revenue of $81.6 billion -- up 85% year over year. The company's core data center segment grew even faster, at 92% year over year, to $75.2 billion. Nvidia's accelerating top line wasn't the only highlight from its earnings report. The company's earnings per share (EPS) increased 214% year over year, while free cash flow rose 86%.\nWhile Palantir's 85% revenue growth during the calendar year 2026's Q1 was on par with that of Nvidia, the company only generated $1.6 billion in sales. Nevertheless, even with a modest revenue base to work from, Palantir has demonstrated an ability to maintain profitability thanks to insatiable demand from both large commercial enterprises and government agencies for its Artificial Intelligence Platform (AIP) -- a suite that stitches together its flagship software programs, Foundry, Gotham, and Apollo. The company's adjusted free cash flow rose 57% year over year, while EPS increased fourfold.\nAll told, Nvidia's growth remains primarily hardware-led and tied to the physical build-out of AI capacity. Meanwhile, Palantir's business is software-led and largely dependent on organizations embedding its tools into operational workflows. While bo…",
  "statement": "While Palantir's 85% revenue growth during the calendar year 2026's Q1 was on par with that of Nvidia, the company only generated $1.6 billion in sales."
 },
 {
  "id": "G089",
  "article_id": "gnews:f8d63eab0337895e8e95cf02",
  "title": "Nvidia Uses The Specter of Huawei to Make Its Chip Exports Case",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-02",
  "article_opening": "Before Nvidia won approval to sell more chips to China, it told the U.S. government that Huawei could have enough chips to satisfy global demand.\nBy Noah Berman\nWhen Nvidia pitched the Trump administration last year to let it sell advanced AI chips to China, the world’s biggest chipmaker issued a clear warning: Huawei, its most formidable Chinese rival, could soon dominate the global industry.\nIn a report that Nvidia presented to the U.S. government — which The Wire has reviewed — the company wa",
  "passage": "… on Capitol Hill by saying things that were incredibly difficult to believe. I hope that would give members of Congress and staffers and the White House pause about taking numbers, and the interpretation of those numbers, at face value.\nGregory Allen , an expert on semiconductor export controls\n“Huawei has no way right now to satisfy global demand,” he adds.\nHuawei has certainly been trying to improve its offering. In March, it introduced a new chip, the 950PR, and is preparing to launch another later this year. Yet even those chips fail to match the quality of the H200 because they have slower memory , according to Heim. They lag even further behind Nvidia’s most advanced Blackwell chips .\nHuawei and SMIC did not respond to requests for comment.\nThe Chinese government has to date locked Nvidia out of the China market, despite the Trump administration having cleared its path for H200 exports.\n“We sell the first, best and most of our technology to America and offer competitive products in China to commercial users vetted and approved by the U.S. government,” says the Nvidia spokesperson. “National security is first and foremost. Where commercial opportunity conflicts with U.S. national security, national security comes first.”\nBut Nvidia is losing sympathy in Washington, says Allen, the analyst.\n“Nvidia has really damaged their credibility on Capitol Hill by saying things that were incredibly difficult to believe,” he says. “I hope t…",
  "statement": "Huawei and SMIC did not respond to requests for comment."
 },
 {
  "id": "G341",
  "article_id": "gnews:56085a9d3e88c464bcaed67b",
  "title": "Develop Humanoid Robot Policies End-to-End with NVIDIA Isaac GR00T | NVIDIA Technical Blog",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-07",
  "article_opening": "By Edith Llontop and Brandon Neel\nThe NVIDIA Isaac GR00T Development Platform unifies every stage of the humanoid robot pipeline, from data collection and model training to large-scale evaluation and deployment readiness.\nGR00T 1.7 is the first open, commercially usable vision-language-action model for generalized humanoid skills, released under the Apache 2.0 license with a 3-billion-parameter base checkpoint.\nThe model introduces a Cosmos-Reason2-2B (Qwen3-VL architecture) backbone, robust pre",
  "passage": "…lse stays the same; you’re only changing how many rollouts you average over.\nThat completes a full pass through the Isaac GR00T Development Platform. Each stage produces a clean artifact that feeds directly into the next for a fully integrated, streamlined development process. While this walkthrough uses a specific embodiment as an example, the same process can be adapted to your own robot setup, define a task for your use case, collect demonstrations on your system, and reuse the same data conversion, post-training, and evaluation workflow.\nA growing GR00T ecosystem\nThe GR00T platform and reference workflow are already being adopted across a growing ecosystem of humanoid robotics partners.\nHumanoid makers and AI providers including 1X, Agility, ANYBotics, Bellboy Robotics, FieldAI, Lightwheel AI, NEURA Robotics, Nexuni, Noble Machines, Schaeffler, Skild AI, and Techman Robot are integrating components of the GR00T platform such as Isaac Teleop, Sim, Lab, and ROS to accelerate their development pipeline and bring AI-enabled robots into real industrial use faster.\nResearch institutions including Stanford, CMU, UCSD, ETH Zurich, and AI2 will be experimenting with the unified GR00T end-to-end workflow to reduce integration complexity and move faster from robot bring-up to skill development and real-world validation.\nLeading wearable and XR device makers including Haptikos, Manus, Noitom, OpenGraph Labs, PICO, Sensing, HTC Vive, Wuji, and Xsens offer devices with native support for NVIDIA Isaac Teleop, making it easier to capture high-quality demonstrations.\nThe GR00T platform and GR00T 1.7 model are available tod…",
  "statement": "Humanoid makers and AI providers including 1X, Agility, ANYBotics, Bellboy Robotics, FieldAI, Lightwheel AI, NEURA Robotics, Nexuni, Noble Machines, Schaeffler, Skild AI, and Techman Robot are integrating components of the GR00T platform"
 },
 {
  "id": "G213",
  "article_id": "gnews:71df54965281684cc500960f",
  "title": "Nvidia Stock Is Below $200 Per Share Again. Here's When It Could Hit $300.",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "For a long time, Nvidia ( NVDA +2.48% ) was priced above $200 per share. Now, it has fallen below that after a few days of heavy selling pressure. The stock is down around 16% from its highs and is up a mere 6% for the year. That's a pretty disappointing result for most investors since Nvidia has been such a strong stock pick over the past few years.\nHowever, I still think the stock still has a bright future. In fact, I think it could easily rise over 50% to reach $300. But when might that occur",
  "passage": "…dominant force in the industry\nThe most recent round of artificial intelligence (AI) sell-offs has come from a familiar source: fear of overspending. As the market becomes worried that AI hyperscalers are spending too much on their buildout, any stock associated with the industry is struggling.\nNvidia is not isolated from that, which is why it has sold off so much. However, time and time again, the hyperscalers have told investors that the risk of underspending far outweighs the risk of overspending. The market may be growing frustrated by lofty capital expenditures on data centers, but the hyperscalers aren't planning on slowing down anytime soon.\nDuring its last quarterly conference call, management forecast that it expects hyperscaler spending to top $1 trillion in 2027 after reaching a projected $650 billion in 2026 .\nThat sounds like further revenue growth ahead, and that's exactly what Wall Street analysts are projecting as well. For the rest of this year, they expect 82% growth. For 2027, that figure rises to 41%. Those are strong growth rates and will likely lead to major profits as well.\nFor fiscal 2028, ending January 2028, the average analyst projects $12.76 in earnings per share. I think that's a conservative estimate, because the analyst community has consistently underprojected Nvidia's growth.\nIf we use that figure and value the stock at a reasonable earnings multiple of 25, the projected share price at the end of fiscal 2028 is $319. As a result, I think the stock could easily reach $300 pe…",
  "statement": "management forecast that it expects hyperscaler spending to top $1 trillion in 2027 after reaching a projected $650 billion in 2026"
 },
 {
  "id": "G207",
  "article_id": "nvblog:open-models-icml-2026",
  "title": "How Open Models Are Driving AI Research | NVIDIA Blog",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "At the International Conference on Machine Learning, nearly 145 papers cite NVIDIA Nemotron open models and datasets as the foundation for new research.\nEvery year, the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML) reveals where thousands of AI researchers have decided to put their work.\nThis year’s accepted papers reveal a clear direction: open frontier models and open AI infrastructure have become foundational to how modern AI science gets done.\nNVIDIA had 74 papers accepted at ICML 2026",
  "passage": "…interpret and design genetic sequences.\nMerck & Co. , uses KERMT to predict how potential drug molecules may behave in the body, including whether they are likely to be effective, safe and developable.\nSakana AI — attending ICML this year — built its Fugu and Fugu-Ultra models directly on Nemotron 3 Ultra, using the open foundation to push forward its work on AI research automation.\nKiloCode integrated Nemotron into its code-routing architecture, reporting token cost reductions of up to 90% — a result with real implications for the economics of deploying AI in production.\nNAVER developed its own model using the Nemotron architecture, extending the foundation for Korean-language AI research.\nTogether AI is hosting Nemotron models on its platform, making them more accessible to researchers who need reliable, seamless access to open inference.\nHumanoid , LG Electronics , NEURA Robotics and Noble Machines are adopting NVIDIA Isaac GR00T models to accelerate industrial deployments of their humanoids, while 1X , Agility , Agile Robots , Boston Dynamics , Hexagon Robotics , and Mentee are building the next generation of humanoids using Cosmos world models, Isaac Sim and Isaac Lab to accelerate the development and validation of their robots.\nExplore NVIDIA’s open models on Hugging Face .\nExplore genomics and biology research at ICML’s GenBio Workshop on Friday, July 10.",
  "statement": "Together AI is hosting Nemotron models on its platform"
 },
 {
  "id": "G409",
  "article_id": "gnews:bf96def37a9c3a5f19f5d43c",
  "title": "LG Electronics to Build Nvidia AI Server Racks",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-12",
  "article_opening": "By Lee Seok-jin\nLG Electronics (066570.KS) is developing an AI chip-based server rack targeting Nvidia and plans to begin mass production next year. The move expands the recent AI infrastructure alliance between LG and Nvidia into server rack manufacturing.\nAccording to the semiconductor industry on the 12th, LG Electronics' Production Research Institute (PRI) completed development of an AI server rack prototype matching the compute tray specifications of Nvidia's \"Vera Rubin\" in the first half ",
  "passage": "By Lee Seok-jin\nLG Electronics (066570.KS) is developing an AI chip-based server rack targeting Nvidia and plans to begin mass production next year. The move expands the recent AI infrastructure alliance between LG and Nvidia into server rack manufacturing.\nAccording to the semiconductor industry on the 12th, LG Electronics' Production Research Institute (PRI) completed development of an AI server rack prototype matching the compute tray specifications of Nvidia's \"Vera Rubin\" in the first half of the year. LG plans to secure orders from global big tech companies after reliability evaluations in the second half and begin mass production next year. The research and development was conducted at a 1.8-megawatt data center cluster established at LG PRI's Pyeongtaek site.\nA compute tray is a module that integrates computing units such as gr…",
  "statement": "LG Electronics (066570.KS) is developing an AI chip-based server rack targeting Nvidia and plans to begin mass production next year"
 },
 {
  "id": "G476",
  "article_id": "gnews:621e1b678a037049c86a70af",
  "title": "Nvidia vs. Micron: Which Is the Better AI Semiconductor Stock to Own for the Next 5 Years?",
  "publication_day": "2026-09-04",
  "article_opening": "Artificial intelligence (AI) infrastructure stocks are still one of the biggest driving forces in the market. While Nvidia ( NVDA +2.53% ) helped lead the early charge, other stocks, like Micron ( MU +4.20% ) , have greatly outperformed the AI leader over the past year.\nLet's dive into both AI stocks to see which one looks set to outperform over the next five years.\nNvidia was the first big AI winner, and the advantages the company established remain in place today. Meanwhile, despite its massiv",
  "passage": "… year earlier to $25.11.\nThe revenue growth and margin expansion are coming from a huge supply-demand imbalance within the memory market. The company is one of the big three DRAM makers -- along with Korean companies SK Hynix and Samsung -- and derives around three-quarters of its revenue from DRAM and a quarter from NAND (flash). AI is driving memory demand, as GPUs are packaged with high bandwidth memory (HBM), a special form of DRAM that helps reduce latency and optimize performance, and AI training data is being stored in enormous solid state drives (SSDs) that use flash memory.\nRival SK Hynix has predicted that the DRAM market will remain supply-constrained through at least 2030, while Micron and its rivals have begun to lock in long-term contracts for up to five years for the first time ever. This should help reduce some of the extreme cyclicality the industry has seen in the past.\nOn a forward P/E basis, Micron looks very cheap, trading at only 6 times analyst estimates for fiscal 2027 (ending August 2027). The question is how long the memory cycle will last and whether there has been enough of a structural shift in the market to keep prices high.\nI think the easy answer here is that Nvidia will outperform Micron over the next five years. Micron is still a cyclical stock until proven otherwise, and five years is a long time for a cycle to last. Meanwhile, Nvidia has built one of the most complete and powerful AI infrastructure companies on the planet, and the stock is very att…",
  "statement": "Micron and its rivals have begun to lock in long-term contracts for up to five years for the first time ever"
 },
 {
  "id": "G125",
  "article_id": "gnews:e4e5f1b5c0c68381e4c486e1",
  "title": "Here's What a $25,000 Investment in Nvidia Could Be Worth if History Repeats Itself",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-03",
  "article_opening": "The artificial intelligence chip giant Nvidia ( NVDA +2.48% ) has struggled recently and is now down nearly 13% over the past month (as of July 1). After years of an incredible AI rally, doubts are now creeping into investors' minds.\nThere are questions about whether the large tech companies can continue to invest hundreds of billions annually in AI infrastructure, which is driving much of the cycle, and whether constraints such as energy and power will eventually have a real impact. For Nvidia ",
  "passage": "…ch of the cycle, and whether constraints such as energy and power will eventually have a real impact. For Nvidia specifically, competition in the chip space seems to be intensifying.\nThat said, Nvidia hasn't done anything to suggest its business is at risk of slowing, so if the concerns don't materialize, the stock could have upside. Here's what a $25,000 investment in Nvidia could be worth if history repeats itself.\nWhile many high-flying AI companies trade at monster valuations, that's not really something you can say about Nvidia.\nOn the surface, Nvidia trading at roughly 22 times forward earnings looks quite reasonable. In the first quarter of Nvidia's fiscal year 2027, ending April 26, the company grew revenue 85% year over year, while diluted earnings per share soared by 140%.\nIn March, CEO Jensen Huang said he expects the company's Blackwell and Vera Rubin chips to generate $1 trillion in sales between that time and the end of 2027.\nFurthermore, in its first fiscal quarter of 2027, Nvidia announced plans to become much more involved in the central processing unit (CPU) space , which has been in high demand due to the rise of agentic AI. Management said they think they can generate nearly $20 billion of CPU sales, making it an instant industry leader.\nObviously, there are real concerns about Nvidia from an industry perspective and a company-specific perspective.\nFrom an industry perspective, if AI experiences a significant pullback or turns out not to be as cons…",
  "statement": "the company grew revenue 85% year over year, while diluted earnings per share soared by 140%"
 },
 {
  "id": "G246",
  "article_id": "gnews:25d0b1d2a9dfe0bb99432e1a",
  "title": "NVIDIA Falls 7% in a Month: Should You Still Hold NVDA Stock or Exit?",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "/ NVIDIA Falls 7% in a Month: Should You Still Hold NVDA Stock or Exit?\nNVIDIA Falls 7% in a Month: Should You Still Hold NVDA Stock or Exit?\nNVIDIA Corporation NVDA shares have declined 6.6% over the past month, underperforming the broader Zacks Computer and Technology sector, which slipped 2.2% during the same period. At first glance, the decline may look worrying, but a closer look suggests the weakness is more about market sentiment than company-specific problems.\nThe sell-off has been wides",
  "passage": "…lows NVIDIA to invest aggressively in research and development, expand production capacity and reward shareholders. During the quarter, the company repurchased $19.3 billion worth of shares and paid $243 million in dividends.\nNVDA Stock’s Valuation Still Looks Attractive\nDespite becoming one of the world's most valuable companies, NVIDIA's valuation is still very attractive.\nThe stock currently trades at a forward 12-month price-to-earnings (P/E) multiple of 18.87, below the Zacks Computer & Technology sector average of 22.73.\nNVIDIA Forward 12-Month P/E Ratio\nNVIDIA also trades at a lower multiple than several semiconductor peers such as Marvell Technology, STMicroelectronics and Broadcom. Marvell carries a forward P/E of 49.59, STMicroelectronics trades at 33.49, and Broadcom at 21.50.\nThat discount is noteworthy because NVIDIA continues to deliver faster growth, stronger profitability and a more dominant competitive position than many of its peers. If the company maintains its current execution, the valuation still leaves room for long-term upside.\nShould Investors Hold NVIDIA Stock?\nThe recent pullback appears to be driven more by broad semiconductor weakness and concerns over AI spending than by any deterioration in NVIDIA's business. The company continues to dominate the AI infrastructure market, deliver exceptional financial results and generate massive cash flows, while its valuation remains reasonable relative to its growth outlook.\nThe investment thesis remains in…",
  "statement": "Marvell carries a forward P/E of 49.59, STMicroelectronics trades at 33.49, and Broadcom at 21.50."
 },
 {
  "id": "G018",
  "article_id": "gnews:87607a311ad24b410c6ef85a",
  "title": "Intel Price Forecast: Nvidia Picked Xeon 6, Invested $5B, Yet Analysts Still Trail INTC",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-01",
  "article_opening": "Intel Price Forecast: Nvidia Picked Xeon 6, Invested $5B, Yet Analysts Still Trail INTC\nIntel Corporation is demonstrating significant momentum, trading at $140.05 amid robust AI-driven demand. Core catalysts include NVIDIA’s $5 billion equity investment and the selection of Intel’s Xeon 6 for DGX Rubin AI systems, alongside multi-year, volume-locked agreements with Google and Microsoft. Q1 2026 data shows strong revenue growth in Data Center/AI and Foundry segments, with AI-linked revenue reach",
  "passage": "…he companies also are partnering on custom-designed ASIC Infrastructure Processing Unit (IPU) chips that will help boost utilization, lower system complexity, and scale more efficiently for AI tasks.\nCEO Tan said the agreements span three to five years, with locked volume and pricing. That represents a rare and tangible commitment in an industry to provide revenue visibility for the term. The structure directly addresses the main criticism of Intel's revenue run-rate so far this year, which is that demand is not real but order-driven (front-loaded).\nAdd to the list of commitments NVIDIA's $5B equity commitment plus Xeon 6 product selection, Google's multi-year Xeon and custom ASIC deal, and Microsoft's previously confirmed 18A wafer deal, plus Apple foundry negotiations that are ongoing.\nThis deal stack is where the current price stands relative to the average price target of $93.93 on the stock from analyst coverage. This gap represents how fast the new Intel story is unfolding versus the rate at which sell-side analysts are updating their models: Cantor Fitzgerald raised its price target to $150 from $90 in early July 1 and still held a Neutral rating noting its price target remains a mere 13.9% above current levels.\nCurrent valuation checks (per Simply Wall St) show Intel trades at a price-to-sales multiple of 13.1x vs. a suggested fair price multiple of 15.1x given its combination of foundry + AI exposure, and scores 2 of 6 on its broader valuation checks. While INTC i…",
  "statement": "Microsoft's previously confirmed 18A wafer deal, plus Apple foundry negotiations that are ongoing"
 },
 {
  "id": "G437",
  "article_id": "gnews:57de52b4f33b0c903d256f9e",
  "title": "DeepSeek Just Dealt Nvidia Stock a Potentially Major Blow",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-09",
  "article_opening": "DeepSeek has already rattled Nvidia Corporation (NVDA) investors once with its low-cost artificial intelligence (AI) models. Now, the Chinese startup may be posing a far more direct competitive threat. Reports that DeepSeek is developing its own inference-focused AI chip could reduce its reliance on Nvidia's GPUs, highlighting a broader industry trend in which leading AI developers are increasingly designing custom silicon to lower costs, improve efficiency, and gain greater control over their A",
  "passage": "…igh of $236.54 reached on May 14. Investor sentiment has been pressured by concerns over the sustainability of AI infrastructure spending, intensifying competition, U.S. export restrictions, and uncertainty surrounding the rollout of next-generation AI platforms, including Rubin and Kyber.\nRecently, Nvidia faced reports that suggested its next-generation Kyber rack-scale AI infrastructure could be delayed until 2028. However, the company denied the speculation, maintaining that its roadmap remains on track. Designed for the Rubin Ultra platform, Kyber is expected to launch in 2027 as an integrated AI infrastructure system and a key component of Nvidia's long-term AI infrastructure strategy.\nNvidia trades at 22.49 times forward earnings, which is currently a discount compared to industry peers and the historical average.\nNvidia delivered another exceptional quarter when it reported first-quarter fiscal 2027 results on May 20, further cementing its leadership in the rapidly growing AI infrastructure market.\nFor the quarter ended April 26, Nvidia posted record revenue of $81.6 billion, an 85% year-over-year (YOY) increase, while net income jumped 211% YOY to $58.32 billion. On a non-GAAP basis, earnings per share (EPS) rose 140% from the prior-year period to $1.87, beating analyst estimates. Non-GAAP gross margin expanded to 75% from 60.8% a year earlier, underscoring the company's strong pricing power and favorable AI product mix.\nMoreover, the Data Center segment remained Nvidia's biggest growth driver, wit…",
  "statement": "Nvidia trades at 22.49 times forward earnings, which is currently a discount compared to industry peers and the historical average."
 },
 {
  "id": "G302",
  "article_id": "gnews:417f6244f0aaa27123023deb",
  "title": "A Utah microreactor just powered an Nvidia AI chip live on stage, a first for next-generation nuclear in America, and the two companies used the moment to announce a 30-megawatt AI factory in the desert designed to drink zero local water",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Valar Atomics just made history in the Utah desert by connecting a next-generation nuclear reactor directly to an Nvidia AI chip — a first in America — and the implications for the data center industry's biggest PR crisis go far beyond one desktop PC drawing power on a stage.\nThe reactor cooling itself with pressurized helium instead of water is the quiet technical detail that changes everything about where AI infrastructure can be built next.\nWith over $130 billion in data center projects alrea",
  "passage": "…arguing the NRC lacks licensing authority over some microreactors, according to Reuters. The company would rather answer to states.\nAnd every performance figure from the stage came from Valar itself. Nobody independent has audited that 37%, or the 100 kilowatts, yet.\nThe 30-megawatt plan is the actual story\nAlongside the demo, the two companies announced an agreement to jointly explore nuclear-powered AI systems. The concrete version under study is a 30-megawatt closed-loop AI factory in Utah, which Valar’s own announcement says “consumes no water from the local community.”\nThe trick is stacking two waterless technologies. The reactor already cools itself with helium. On the computing side, Nvidia says its DSX facility design cuts cooling water from roughly 2.6 million gallons per megawatt per year to near zero, with warm-water loops replacing the usual chillers.\nRun Nvidia’s own number against a conventional 30 MW site and you land around 78 million gallons a year. The pitch here is zero of it, in a desert.\n“Through this work with Valar Atomics, Nvidia is exploring how behind-the-meter, waterless advanced nuclear systems could support future AI factories,” John Josephakis, a global vice president at Nvidia, told Reuters.\nNote the verb. Exploring. What nobody has published is a construction schedule, an investment figure, or a permit. FOX 13 reported the future Emery County factory drew big cheers at the event, and has no timeline attached.\nValar itself is a 2023-founded outfit from El Segundo, Ca…",
  "statement": "Nvidia says its DSX facility design cuts cooling water from roughly 2.6 million gallons per megawatt per year to near zero"
 },
 {
  "id": "G202",
  "article_id": "nvblog:open-models-icml-2026",
  "title": "How Open Models Are Driving AI Research | NVIDIA Blog",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "At the International Conference on Machine Learning, nearly 145 papers cite NVIDIA Nemotron open models and datasets as the foundation for new research.\nEvery year, the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML) reveals where thousands of AI researchers have decided to put their work.\nThis year’s accepted papers reveal a clear direction: open frontier models and open AI infrastructure have become foundational to how modern AI science gets done.\nNVIDIA had 74 papers accepted at ICML 2026",
  "passage": "At the International Conference on Machine Learning, nearly 145 papers cite NVIDIA Nemotron open models and datasets as the foundation for new research.\nEvery year, the International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML) reveals where thousands of AI researchers have decided to put their work.\nThis year’s accepted papers reveal a clear direction: open frontier models and open AI infrastructure have become foundational to how modern AI science gets done.\nNVIDIA had 74 papers accepted at ICML 2026. Approximately 2,000 accepted papers cite NVIDIA GPUs, and 145 cite NVIDIA Nemotron — a family of open models , including open datasets — as the foundation for new research. Hundreds more draw on NVIDIA Cosmos , NVIDIA Isaac GR00T , BioNeMo and other NVIDIA open model families, spanning physical AI, robotics, autonomous vehicles and biomedical research.\nThe Themes Defining This Year’s Research\nAreas including vision and video generation, reinforcement learning for large language models ( LLMs ) and agent training as well as AI inference remained prominent themes across this year’s papers, reflecting sustained investment these fields command — while several new areas also broke through.\nRobot world models drew significant attention, with papers like DreamDojo pushing the bo…",
  "statement": "NVIDIA had 74 papers accepted at ICML 2026. Approximately 2,000 accepted papers cite NVIDIA GPUs, and 145 cite NVIDIA Nemotron"
 },
 {
  "id": "G259",
  "article_id": "gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32",
